'''Mário Sá "Mar" Justino''' (16 May 1921{{snd}}10 October 2009) was a [[Balisca|Baliscan]] politician, engineer and guerrilla fighter, that served as the #th [[Secretary General of Balisca]]. He was a founding member of the [[Revolutionary Front]] (FR), and was a firebrand [[W:Trotskyism|Trotskyist]]–[[W:Posadist|Posadist]] that called for [[W:Nuclear proliferation|nuclear proliferation]] and [[W:World revolution|world revolution]]. As Secretary General, Justino played a strong role in {{W|international diplomacy|international matters}} including activities related to the {{W|Non-Aligned Movement}}, being described as "a man with audacious ambitions to alter the balance of power among nations". He additionally oversaw the intensification of the [[Balisca and weapons of mass destruction|Baliscan nuclear program]] in the face of [[Camboriú War|Potocsino aggression]] and the [[Gulf of Guinea Crisis]]. 

Justino's government was criticized within Balisca, particularly for the decision to [[1977 Baliscan confederal election#Background|postpone the 1976 elections]], and its heavy-handed response to subsequent domestic unrest. The mass demonstrations and strikes in Balisca in [[October 76|October 1976]] severely challenged Justino legitimacy, as he called for the [[W:Riot control|breaking up]] of student protests against [[Confederal emergency|restrictive emergency measures]] during the Camboriú War. This prompted [[W:Solidarity action|solidarity actions]] by [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]], ultimately leading to a [[W:General strike|general strike]] and street clashes between protestors (supported by [[Autonomous Militia|autonomous militias]]) and the [[Guardia Civil (Balisca)|Guardia Civil]], splitting the armed forces and disrupting the confederal response to the Potoscino aggression. On 28 December, the [[Confederal Congress]] (including the majority of the Revolutionary Front) voted to [[W:Censured|censure]] Justino, and ordered the Guardia Civil to stand down.

The October 1977 elections were a major defeat for the FR and their allies; in the face of increasing authoritarianism and worsening economic conditions, the electorate swung significantly towards [[Anarchism in Balisca|anti-authoritarians]]. The platform's support cratered across the confederation, managing to elect only 36 delegates (out of 718 seats). For the rest of his tenure, Justino remained personally unpopular; a survey conducted immediately after the crisis showed that a majority of the country saw him as irrational, too self-centered, too authoritarian, and too aggressive.

Justino's legacy is controversial, but he is recognized as a powerful figure in the Baliscan political landscape, particularly for his strides towards {{W|global justice}}, his modernization policies, and his {{W|anti-imperialist}} efforts. His premiership also encouraged and coincided with a Baliscan cultural boom, and the launching of large industrial projects, including the continued expansion of [[AVB|high speed rail in Balisca]].  

